###["### Suggested Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n#### Step 1: Ratio Features\n\nCreating ratio features helps capture meaningful relationships between various attributes and can highlight relative contributions or proportions. Ratios between Halstead and McCabe metrics could be predictive of software defects.\n\npython\n# loc_to_volume_ratio\n# Ratio of lines of code to Halstead 'volume'. Captures density of code per unit volume of information.\n# Usefulness: This feature adds knowledge about how compact the code is relative to its volume, which may correlate with defects due to excessive information density. \n\ndf['loc_to_volume_ratio'] = df['loc'] / (df['v'] + 1e-8)\n\n# complexity_to_operator_ratio\n# Ratio of cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) to total operators. Measures complexity per operator.\n# Usefulness: Modules with high complexity relative to code operations may be error-prone due to convoluted control flows.\n\ndf['complexity_to_operator_ratio'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-8)\n\n# comment_density\n# Ratio of lines of comments (lOComment) to lines of code (lOCode). Indicates documentation quality.\n# Usefulness: Insufficient comment density may lead to defects, as maintainability and comprehension of the code suffer.\n\ndf['comment_density'] = df['lOComment'] / (df['lOCode'] + 1e-8)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Interaction Features\n\nInteraction terms can capture relationships or compound effects between predictors. Mupltiplying key Halstead and McCabe metrics might highlight unexpected dynamics.\n\npython\n# loc_with_cyclomatic_complexity\n# Product of lines of code (loc) and cyclomatic complexity (v(g)). Measures the impact of size and complexity.\n# Usefulness: Larger, more complex modules have a higher likelihood of defects due to increased risk factors.\n\ndf['loc_with_cyclomatic_complexity'] = df['loc'] * df['v(g)']\n\n# difficulty_with_intelligence\n# Product of Halstead difficulty (d) and intelligence (i). Captures the interplay between challenge and cognitive effort needed.\n# Usefulness: Modules with higher difficulty but lower intelligence scores may indicate poor software quality.\n\ndf['difficulty_with_intelligence'] = df['d'] * df['i']\n\n# essential_and_design_complexity\n# Sum of essential complexity (ev(g)) and design complexity (iv(g)). Captures combined impact of both complexity dimensions.\n# Usefulness: Higher combined complexity correlates with difficult-to-maintain or error-prone code.\n\ndf['essential_and_design_complexity'] = df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Aggregated Metrics\n\nCombine related metrics into groups to summarize module characteristics. This improves the interpretability and reduces redundancy while retaining signal strength.\n\npython\n# total_complexity\n# Aggregate of cyclomatic, essential, and design complexity (v(g), ev(g), iv(g)). Summarizes module complexity as a single measure.\n# Usefulness: Useful as a holistic indicator of how complex a module is, reducing feature redundancy in downstream models.\n\ndf['total_complexity'] = df['v(g)'] + df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']\n\n# total_operators_and_operands\n# Sum of total operators and total operands (total_Op, total_Opnd). Combines code interaction metrics into one.\n# Usefulness: Condenses the code operation features to focus on overall operator-operand interactions, which are key predictors for Halstead-based defect analysis.\n\ndf['total_operators_and_operands'] = df['total_Op'] + df['total_Opnd']\n\n# code_density\n# Sum of lines of code (lOCode), lines of comments (lOComment), and blank lines (lOBlank). Characterizes code module density.\n# Usefulness: Provides a single feature to capture module footprint, balancing structural and documentation aspects.\n\ndf['code_density'] = df['lOCode'] + df['lOComment'] + df['lOBlank']\nend\n\n#### Dropped Columns\n\nColumns that provide less signal or are redundant with newly engineered features can be dropped to streamline the dataset.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column locCodeAndComment is dropped\n# locCodeAndComment is dropped because the newly created code_density feature already represents a more comprehensive version of this attribute.\ndf.drop(columns=['locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column uniq_Op is dropped\n# uniq_Op is dropped because it shows significant correlation with uniq_Opnd, and total_operators_and_operands condenses these into a broader feature.\ndf.drop(columns=['uniq_Op'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column b is dropped\n# b is dropped because it is the least informative Halstead metric with very small signal strength (mean = 0.0877) and high sparsity.\ndf.drop(columns=['b'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Summary\n\n- Step 1 (Ratios): Focus on creating features that highlight relationships between metrics.\n- Step 2 (Interactions): Combine independent metrics to capture compound effects.\n- Step 3 (Aggregations): Group related attributes into concise features for better model performance and interpretability.\n- Dropped Columns: Remove redundant or less relevant features to ensure cleaner data and reduce noise.\n\nEach of the added features is designed to encapsulate additional information about a module's complexity, maintainability, and quality, which are critical for accurate software defect prediction.", "### Final Solution: Three Feature Engineering Steps for KC1 Software Defect Prediction\n\nThe goal is to improve downstream model performance by creating intuitive, impactful features and dropping redundant columns. \n\n#### Step 1: Create a Ratio Between Cyclomatic Complexity and Lines of Code\npython\n# Feature name: complexity_per_loc\n# Feature description: Ratio of McCabe's cyclomatic complexity (v(g)) to lines of code (loc). Represents the complexity of the code per unit size.\n# Usefulness: Modules with higher complexity per code line may be more error-prone due to convoluted control flows. Capturing this ratio reflects how much complexity is concentrated in the code.\n\ndf['complexity_per_loc'] = df['v(g)'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Sum of Unique Operands and Operators\npython\n# Feature name: total_unique_elements\n# Feature description: Sum of unique operands (uniq_Opnd) and unique operators (uniq_Op). Represents the diversity or richness of the code module in terms of distinct elements.\n# Usefulness: Modules using a wide range of operands and operators may indicate complexity or higher functionality, which could impact defect likelihood.\n\ndf['total_unique_elements'] = df['uniq_Opnd'] + df['uniq_Op']\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Log Transformation of Halstead's Volume (v)\npython\n# Feature name: log_volume\n# Feature description: Logarithm transformation of Halstead's volume (v). Reduces skewness in highly variable feature values, making them suitable for modeling.\n# Usefulness: Halstead's volume is associated with code size and information density, which may correlate with defects. The log transformation ensures stability for statistical models.\n\ndf['log_volume'] = np.log1p(df['v'])  # Use log1p to handle zero values\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped Columns\n\n#### Drop Columns With Very Low Variance or Limited Predictive Value\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOBlank is dropped\n# The lOBlank column has minimal variance and contains blank line counts, which do not provide a meaningful signal for defect prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOBlank'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column b is dropped\n# The b column is a Halstead metric with low information content and distribution sparsity (mean = 0.0877, Std = 0.1800), making it unlikely to contribute to defect prediction.\ndf.drop(columns=['b'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column lOComment is dropped\n# The lOComment column has a median and Q3 value of 0.0 and limited variance, indicating it is unlikely to be a significant predictor for defects.\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Summary\n\n- Step 1: Focuses on capturing complexity relative to the size of the code module.\n- Step 2: Adds a measure of the diversity of code elements (unique operands and operators).\n- Step 3: Reduces skewness in Halstead volume, ensuring better model interpretability and stability.\n- Dropped Columns: Removed features with limited variance and predictive power to streamline the dataset.", "### Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\n#### Step 1: Ratio-based Feature for Complexity Normalization\npython\n# Feature name: normalized_complexity\n# Feature description: Ratio of total complexity (v(g) + ev(g) + iv(g)) to lines of code (loc). Captures how complexity scales per unit of code size.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing the total complexity by the size of the module helps identify modules that are disproportionately complex relative to their size, which can be indicative of potential defects.\n\ndf['normalized_complexity'] = df['total_complexity'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-8)\nend\n\n#### Step 2: Interaction Feature Between Operators and Operands\npython\n# Feature name: operator_operand_interaction\n# Feature description: Product of total operators (total_Op) and total operands (total_Opnd). Represents the degree of interaction between operators and operands.\n# Usefulness: Modules with frequent and complex operator-operand interactions could have a higher chance of defects due to the increased cognitive load on developers.\n \ndf['operator_operand_interaction'] = df['total_Op'] * df['total_Opnd']\nend\n\n#### Step 3: Ratio Feature for Volume and Total Operators\npython\n# Feature name: volume_per_operator\n# Feature description: Ratio of Halstead 'volume' (v) to total operators (total_Op). Measures the amount of information per operation.\n# Usefulness: High information volume relative to the number of operations may indicate that individual operations are packed with logic, increasing the likelihood of defects.\n\ndf['volume_per_operator'] = df['v'] / (df['total_Op'] + 1e-8)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped Columns for Simplification\n\n#### Column 1: b (Halstead measure)\npython\n# Explanation why the column b is dropped\n# The column b has a very small mean value (0.0877), high sparsity, and weak correlation to the target variable. Its contribution to defect prediction is minimal.\ndf.drop(columns=['b'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column 2: l (Halstead program length)\npython\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\n# The column l has a very small range ([0.0, 2.0]) and provides redundant information about Halstead measures which are better captured by other metrics like volume (v).\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n#### Column 3: log_effort (transformed Halstead effort)\npython\n# Explanation why the column log_effort is dropped\n# The log_effort column is redundant because it is derived from the Halstead 'effort' (e). Additionally, it provides overlapping information with features such as volume or difficulty (d).\ndf.drop(columns=['log_effort'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n1. Added Features: Three new features (normalized_complexity, operator_operand_interaction, volume_per_operator) were created to capture relative scaling, interactions, and density of module metrics, enhancing defect prediction.\n2. Dropped Columns: Columns with minimal or redundant information (b, l, log_effort) were removed for simplification and efficient model training."]###,
